  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Scattered showers possible tonight and dry during \nthe day Monday, though cooler with few clouds following a backdoor \ncold front.\n\nCloud cover continues to build in today as high pressure continues \nto push eastward and eventually offshore. Weak low pressure tracks \nnear the region tonight as well, increasing chances for some \nscattered showers towards CT, RI, SE MA, Cape Cod, and the Islands. \nNot anticipating any washouts with any that move through tonight, \nand these should clear the region heading into Monday morning. Lows \ntonight will generally fall into the upper 50s and low to mid 60s.\n\nA backdoor cold front is expected to make its way across southern \nNew England late tonight into tomorrow morning, clearing skies and \nushering in drier air. Winds aloft shift more N then NE after \nmidnight, reaching the surface after daybreak. This onshore flow \nwill also aid in keeping eastern parts of the region slightly \ncooler; highs closest to the coast will generally be in the low 70s. \nStill, much of the region can expect a less humid day with highs in \nthe upper 70s. Heading into Monday evening/night, high pressure \nshifts full offshore, which will introduce the start of a more \nunsettled pattern.KEY MESSAGE 2...Backdoor cold front Monday \nbrings onshore flow, decreased cloud cover and lower \ntemperatures.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Unsettled Monday night through Thursday, with \nseveral opportunites for showers and thunderstorms. Timing which\ndays may have better storm chances is still low-confidence. \n\nWe still look to enter into a cloudy and unsettled weather pattern, \nas a stalled frontal boundary in the vicinity of Southern New \nEngland or just to our southwest focuses several opportunites for \nshowers and storms Monday night through Thursday. The wavy frontal \nboundary separates hot, humid and conditionally unstable air to our \nsouthwest, with more seasonable and less humid air over \nnorthern/eastern New England, with strong NW flow aloft. It likely \nwon't be raining the whole period of time, but in a pattern governed \nby convection and hard-to-time shortwaves in the NW flow aloft, \ntiming which period(s) and the location of showers and storms is \nstill subject to change and is low-confidence, with poor run-to-run \nconsistency across the model suite. \n\nRight now the two periods that appear to have the best chance of \nshowers and storms looks to be Monday night and into the first part \nof Tuesday, and again later Wednesday afternoon into part of \nThursday. Tried to introduce higher PoPs (high Chance to Likely) for \nthese days, but a general slight chance to chance governs the Monday \nnight thru Thursday period. It looks like the potential for storms \nto become strong to severe looks limited, but a slightly better \nchance exists later Wednesday into Thursday as a stronger, more-\ncoherent shortwave aloft allows for at least some of the more \nunstable warm sector to work its way in. Temperatures were kept in \nthe 70s to low 80s Tue thru Thurs, with rather mild lows in the mid \n60s (climo low temperature values are in the upper 50s/around 60). \n  \nKEY MESSAGE 3...Mix of clouds and sun with diurnally driven \nspot showers Friday into the early part of Labor Day Weekend, \nbut drier weather most of the time. Dry and seasonable Sunday \nand on the Monday holiday, with onshore breezes. \n\nStalled frontal boundary finally moves offshore Thursday, although \ncyclonic flow aloft will keep our weather pattern somewhat unsettled \nwith a mix of clouds and sun. Spot showers are possble Friday and \nSaturday afternoon but should be drier much of the time. Broad high \npressure then works in from the north Sunday into Labor Day, leading \nto an onshore flow but with sunny and dry conditions.",
